From f2673d860e74b8cb8b5384a856a48ab9513c15c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "kevin.krausse" <kkrausse97@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 04:39:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] ggml-webgpu: request only the limits in use, report
 shader/device errors instead of aborting in WASM

The device was created with the adapter's whole limits struct: in a browser
that sends every limit key emdawnwebgpu knows (a browser that does not know
one rejects requestDevice) and asks for far more than is used. Now only
maxStorageBufferBindingSize and maxBufferSize are raised (each at most 1 GiB
and at most what the adapter has); the 1-D workgroup size is capped at 256,
the spec default, which is also faster than 1024 here (110m 56 s encoder
91.7 -> 82.3 ms native browser-like). Limits are read back from the device,
so alignments are the device's. GGML_WEBGPU_LIMITS=default requests nothing
(a spec-default device): every model gives the same text there.

Buffers are split at 256 MiB (GGML_WEBGPU_CHUNK_MB), the default
maxBufferSize; the compute buffer cache keeps several chunks.

Every pipeline is created inside a validation error scope; a failure is
recorded with the pipeline label, the message and getCompilationInfo lines.
In WASM uncaptured errors and device loss are recorded, not aborted on:
ggml_backend_webgpu_last_error() / pk_gpu_errors(). GGML_WEBGPU_DUMP_SHADERS
writes each preprocessed WGSL source; GGML_WEBGPU_BREAK_SHADER=<label> breaks one
to exercise the report.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
